## Problem

Moo has the canonical fact-production, shadow, and freshness issues, but unlike the Moose programme it has no focused production cutover/duplicate-retirement leaf equivalent to #7797.

Today useful Moo behavior is assembled from overlapping paths including:

- `SymbolExtractor` framework synthesis;
- `ClassModelBuilder` attribute/inheritance/role interpretation;
- semantic-analyzer generated-member extraction;
- workspace generated-member extraction;
- completion-local package source parsing / class-model traversal;
- type-inference-owned class/accessor refresh;
- hover/definition special handling;
- role diagnostics consuming their own local class-model projection.

That can preserve good UX during migration, but the admitted static Moo cohort cannot be called canonical while two or more independent exact producers remain live.

## Objective

After #6823–#6827 establish current canonical Moo facts and shadow parity, migrate selected production read-only/type consumers to those facts, remove or explicitly bound superseded Moo interpretation, and prove current external behavior plus work reduction.

Target route:

```text
current source/project/environment
→ exact checked Moo activation
→ one canonical attribute/generated/constructor/role/inheritance/return fact set
→ ProjectModel / semantic queries / type inputs
→ ordinary providers
```

Everything outside the admitted static cohort keeps a named bounded fallback/refusal path. Do not merge contradictory legacy exact output into canonical exact output.

## Admitted consumer classes

Promote only rows already proven by #6826/#6827 and the semantic-correction fixtures, including as applicable:

- generated reader/writer/accessor/predicate/clearer completion;
- admitted static delegated member completion;
- rich hover metadata for source-backed attributes/generated members and referenced hooks;
- definition to the real `has`/role generator anchor;
- constructor-input projections already represented canonically;
- #8957 accessor-return facts into #7280/#7427/#7438;
- inherited and role-provided methods through the canonical object/role query path;
- document/workspace symbols and references only for fact classes with complete current identity/occurrence proof.

`#7484` remains the only generated Moo edit-family owner. This issue does not authorize rename or safe delete.

## Cutover rule

For each promoted consumer/fact class require:

```text
exact current request/source/project identity
+ canonical Moo fact/result
+ passing #2373 semantic truth row
+ passing #6826 live/canonical shadow row
+ passing #6827 edit/freshness row
+ provider-readiness policy
→ canonical production response
```

If any condition is absent, retain the explicitly named previous bounded route or a typed not-ready/dynamic/refusal result.

## Duplicate-path inventory

Inventory every live Moo interpretation and give it exactly one terminal disposition:

```text
removed_after_cutover
retained_standalone_test_only
retained_bounded_fallback_for_named_unsupported_form
transferred_to_canonical_owner
```

At minimum audit:

- `crates/perl-semantic-analyzer/src/analysis/class_model.rs` production uses;
- semantic generated-member extractor;
- workspace generated-member extractor;
- completion workspace/package reparsing and framework-local member collection;
- hover attribute special cases;
- definition/reference generated-member special cases;
- type-inference `ClassModelBuilder` / accessor refresh;
- role diagnostic local Moo facts where canonical relations are available.

No promoted fact class may keep two independent exact producers.

## Type-engine cutover

Coordinate with #7280 and #8957:

- production type inference consumes canonical accessor/method-return inputs;
- no production `ClassModelBuilder::new().build(ast)` scan is performed merely to recover promoted Moo facts;
- core-Moo `isa` package-name guessing is absent;
- direct default/builder return evidence retains its canonical fact/provenance;
- standalone AST inference may remain explicit/test-only and cannot satisfy production provider proof.

## Inheritance and roles

Do not preserve provider-local source parsing merely to traverse `extends`/`with`.

For promoted classes:

- use canonical package/object/MRO and role-composition facts;
- exact DFS/C3 ordering requires complete-enough current graph evidence;
- role provision, role requirement, and role conflict remain distinct;
- dynamic/missing/ambiguous parents/roles degrade rather than becoming exact;
- generated parent/role members retain their original generator anchors.

#6326/#6672 own the common MRO contract; this issue is a consumer, not another linearization implementation.

## Provider behavior

### Completion

- canonical exact receiver/member identities drive the promoted Moo cohort;
- generated methods carry generated/framework detail without becoming explicit-source facts;
- exact own/inherited/role ranking follows canonical object/composition identity;
- fallback remains available for unpromoted/dynamic/not-ready shapes.

### Hover

- preserve or improve the current rich Moo attribute card during cutover;
- attribute mode, constructor metadata, hook metadata, return/type evidence, generator anchor, producer, source tier, and limitations come from canonical facts;
- never replace a richer truthful card with a generic generated-symbol card merely to claim cutover.

### Definition/references

- generated definition targets the real `has`/role generator anchor;
- no fictional generated method body

Parent compiler cache programme: #2462
Depends on live/persistent cache: #7946 / #7947
Compiler identity/invalidation: #2353 / #5241 / #2425
Provider/currentness: #4799 / #3099 / #4241
Crash/storage substrate: #7891 / #4911
Performance authority: #5216 / #1373
Project snapshot composition: #4245

## Objective

Prove the compiler cache as one exact composed product:

```text
current compiler inputs
→ live artifact reuse and interface-aware invalidation
→ optional durable checkpoint
→ fresh-process historical load
→ current key/source/interface/dependency validation
→ canonical compiler-world adoption or recomputation
→ current provider result
```

This is the correctness/currentness/operability closeout gate for #2462. It does not add cache, compiler, provider, storage, or performance semantics.

## Correctness oracle

For every passing artifact/profile row, compare with an independent uncached cold compiler run over the exact current source/config/environment/profile.

Equivalence covers, as applicable:

```text
flat compile facts
HIR/PIR-A identities and normalized content
compile state/effects
module graph/SCC schedule
public compile interface
source maps/provenance/limitations/dynamic boundaries
project/compiler fact projection
external provider response identity/range/content
```

Byte identity is required only where the canonical deterministic codec defines it. Semantic/current external equivalence is required for every admitted row.

A cache hit, artifact count, successful decode, or non-empty provider response is not a pass.

## Required lifecycle matrix

### 1. Cold build and live reuse

- build one exact compiler generation from source;
- populate admitted #7946 artifacts;
- repeat compiler/provider operations;
- prove current hits avoid the corresponding stage work;
- prove hit and forced recomputation produce equivalent compiler/provider results;
- exercise eviction and repopulation without semantic change.

### 2. Private implementation edit

- change a sub/body implementation without changing #5241 compile interface;
- owning module implementation artifacts recompute;
- downstream artifacts remain valid where complete keys permit;
- provider facts remain current or recompute according to their exact dependencies;
- record avoided reverse closure and first-current-answer behavior.

### 3. Public interface changes

Independently change:

- added/removed public sub;
- prototype;
- constant value;
- export/tag;
- parent/`@ISA`/role composition;
- feature/version/pragma/effect contract;
- framework-generated public member.

Prove exact phase/fact-class reverse closure invalidation and provider currentness.

### 4. Dependency and SCC transitions

- add/remove compile-time import edge;
- change runtime-only `require` edge;
- change module resolution/root/environment;
- stable cyclic SCC edit;
- SCC membership/schedule change;
- delete/rename/re-add module;
- multi-root same module names.

Unknown/incomplete dependency state must produce conservative invalidation/miss, not optimistic reuse.

### 5. Dynamic boundary transitions

- widen a static fact to dynamic/unknown;
- narrow/retire a dynamic boundary where earned;
- change confidence/completeness relevant to dependents;
- prove prior exact downstream/provider facts cannot survive a widened boundary.

### 6. Durable restart

- checkpoint one accepted compiler manifest through #7947;
- terminate and start a fresh exact process;
- validate store/manifest/artifact closure;
- validate current source/profile/environment/schema/interface/dependency identity;
- adopt through #7946 or recompute;
- compare with independent cold compiler/provider results;
- prove actual stage work avoided.

### 7. Changed-input restart

Change independently before restart:

```text
source implementation
public interface
dependency/module graph
configuration/environment/profile
HIR/PIR/interface schema or compiler version
framework/adapter identity
```

Prove exact artifacts are reused, rejected, or conservatively recomputed according to their complete keys and dependencies.

### 8. Crash and corruption

Run logical/real-process fault cases against directory reference and selected production backend:

- object write interruption;
- manifest write interruption;
- current-pointer publication interruption;
- process kill before/after commit acknowledgement;
- corrupt/truncated artifact;
- manifest references missing/wrong-digest artifact;
- unknown codec/schema/profile;
- read-only/permission failure;
- stale ownership/lock;
- busy second process.

Restart outcomes are only:

```text
previous complete compiler generation
new complete compiler generation
typed miss/quarantine followed by cold recomputation
```

A mixed compiler world, current pointer to missing artifacts, stale exact provider answer, or startup hang fails.

### 9. Domain isolation

When Project snapshot and compiler artifacts share local storage primitives:

- Project snapshot corruption/clear does not imply compiler corruption/clear;
- compiler clear/prune does not delete Project snapshot state;
- namespace/object keys cannot collide across domains;
- shared worker contention remains bounded and priority policy is observed;
- one domain’s failed transaction cannot corrupt another complete generation.

### 10. Root/session/process lifecycle

- root removal while compile/cache load/write is held;
- configuration/trust transition superseding compiler work;
- server shutdown with pending compiler checkpoint;
- second host/process under #4910 policy;
- no stale insert/adoption/provider publication after newer root/compiler generation;
- no orphan worker/lock/handle/process.

## Provider utility matrix

For selected compiler-backed provider classes, prove independently:

- exact references/navigation where compiler facts are complete;
- safe rename/edit refusal or success under current complete evidence;
- completion/hover/definition where compiler facts are selected;
- fallback behavior when artifac

Parent: #8114
Depends on declaration containers: #8139
Outline eligibility: #993
Accepted semantic snapshot/publication: #7309
Shared-service consumer: #7221
Historical duplicate workaround to retire: #1519

Current-source basis: `f72c1db75f5282635689ec66a57a20561dba9cea`.

## Problem

The current source-backed document-symbol provider reconstructs hierarchy by:

1. iterating `SymbolTable.symbols`, a name-keyed `HashMap`;
2. regrouping symbols into another `HashMap<ScopeId, Vec<Symbol>>`;
3. finding the first scope whose `location.start` equals a container symbol's start;
4. emitting those scope members as one level of leaf children;
5. removing top-level symbols whose start position also appears among immediate children.

This loses canonical order and ownership. It cannot represent arbitrary-depth structure, treats role/method containers inconsistently, and makes the #1519 dedup pass part of semantic identity. Root output has no total sort, while child output uses kind grouping before source ranges and inherits unstable order on exact ties.

## Objective

Replace the reconstruction path with one recursive projection of #8139's canonical declaration-container graph.

```text
accepted current declaration graph
→ visibility policy from #993
→ recursive source-order projection
→ normalized transport-neutral document-symbol tree
→ LSP conversion
```

Every admitted source declaration is projected exactly once from its declaration identity.

## Projection contract

### Input

Consume the current accepted semantic snapshot from #7309, including:

- exact document instance/generation/source identity;
- declaration identity and kind;
- explicit parent/container relation;
- optional owned body container;
- current source range;
- provenance/completeness;
- source-order key.

Do not read the name-keyed `SymbolTable.symbols` map to infer presentation order or ownership.

### Visibility

- Use #993 `outline_visible()` or its final canonical equivalent as the coarse declaration-family gate.
- Fine LSP `SymbolKind`, detail, and presentation remain projector policy.
- Invisible structural containers may be traversed so their visible descendants reach the nearest visible ancestor.
- Source-backed, recovered, generated, and dynamic provenance remain distinct; this PR projects the source-backed class and leaves recovery admission to its dedicated child.

### Recursion

Support arbitrary-depth declaration structure. Container-capable families include the source-backed variants admitted by the semantic model, including:

- package, class, role;
- subroutine and method;
- phase block, labeled statement, format, and other outline-visible body declarations;
- leaves such as fields, package variables, constants, and admitted attributes.

A visible declaration with a body recursively projects the declarations owned by that body. A leaf never gains children by coincident position.

### Ordering

Default order is source order at every level. Use one total comparison independent of map iteration:

```text
source start
source end
declaration-kind discriminator
stable declaration identity
```

The exact stable key may differ, but two declarations must never compare equal unless they are the same identity.

Do not preserve the current kind-first child grouping accidentally. Any future grouped presentation requires a separate explicit product decision.

### Identity and deduplication

- One declaration identity produces at most one visible node.
- Parentage comes only from the canonical container relation.
- Duplicate display names remain distinct when their declaration identities differ.
- Reopened packages remain distinct source declarations unless a later reviewed presentation explicitly groups them.
- No `(line, character)`, byte offset, name, kind, or range tuple is sufficient identity by itself.

## Cleanup

Remove or make unreachable from live document symbols:

- same-start scope search;
- `symbols_by_scope` reconstruction from unordered symbol maps;
- one-level `source_backed_leaf_symbol` hierarchy construction;
- root/child deduplication by child start position introduced for #1519;
- root output whose order depends on `HashMap` seeding.

Preserve #1519's user outcome through the stronger invariant: every declaration identity is projected once.

## Fixtures

- two statement packages and two block packages;
- package/class/role → method/subroutine → nested visible declarations at three or more levels;
- methods and subroutines with equivalent body shapes;
- roles and classes with equivalent member shapes;
- phaser containing a package-scoped named subroutine;
- invisible lexical containers with visible descendants;
- duplicate names under different parents;
- repeated/reopened package declarations;
- declarations sharing start offsets through nested syntax;
- leading shebang/comment/POD metamorphs;
- randomized insertion order of equivalent input records;
- Unicode and mixed-newline source.

## Determinism proof

For an identical semantic snapshot:

- repeated projections in one process are byte-identical;
- projections from separately spawned processes are byte-identical despite randomized hash seeds;
- randomizing input record/map insertion order produces the same tree;
- adding/removing leading trivia changes only ranges, never logical parent paths, kinds, names, or sibling order;
- every flattened declaration identity appears exactly once.

## Negative controls

Tests fail when:

- a scope start offset changes parentage;
- a declaration appears both at root and beneath a parent;
- root or child order changes after input map shuffling;
- a method/role loses children that an equivalent subroutine/class retains;
- recursion stops after one level;
- the #1519 positional dedup pass is required to make tests green;
- a stale/non-current semantic snapshot is projected as current;
- LSP conversion changes semantic order or identity.

## Acceptance

- [ ] Live document symbols consume #8139 declaration containers
